  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The quadratic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=-5,{x}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $1.$ Find $P(x).$\n\n$P(x)=$ {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Since our quadratic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-5$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}) & =(1)(x-(-5))(x-4) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)(x-4) & ={x}^{2}-4x+5x-20 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+x-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{2}+x-20.$",
